WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.580
Hello, Steve Kaufmann here and I'm very
excited to have today as a guest Francesco

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:13.080
uh, and he's going to explain a little
bit about his background because he is

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.570
one of the sort of expanding number of
people on the internet who are creating

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.030
marvelous content in different languages.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.780
And very often at that sort
of intermediate level, which

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.320
is exactly where in so many
languages we are missing content.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.620
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.030
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.830
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.330
Bongiorno.

00:00:39.589 --> 00:00:40.629
Bongiorno Francesco.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:42.449
Bongiorno.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.519
Ciao Steve.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:46.605
We're going to speak in English
because I think it's easier

00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:48.165
uh, more people understand.

00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:53.565
And I think because I want more people to
be aware of what FRansesco is doing and

00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:55.785
how much fun it can be to learn Italian.

00:00:56.235 --> 00:01:01.215
Francesco, could you begin by giving
us a little background about who you

00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:04.395
are and how you got interested in
the work that you're doing and some

00:01:04.395 --> 00:01:05.515
description of what you're doing?

00:01:06.845 --> 00:01:07.345
Sure.

00:01:07.485 --> 00:01:10.244
Uh, first of all, thank you,
Steve, for having me here.

00:01:10.274 --> 00:01:11.384
It's an honor for me.

00:01:12.524 --> 00:01:17.075
And as you said I have a YouTube channel
called Vaporetto Italiano where I

00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:19.544
teach the Italian language and culture.

00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:26.265
And actually, uh, my background
is a bit complicated because

00:01:26.324 --> 00:01:27.615
I didn't start as a teacher.

00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:32.445
And sorry for my English I need to
brush it up a little bit, but I'll try.

00:01:32.535 --> 00:01:33.465
I'll do my best.

00:01:33.524 --> 00:01:34.304
I promise.

00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:42.495
Um, I didn't start as a teacher cause I
studied economy, uh, at university and

00:01:42.495 --> 00:01:47.115
I started working as an export manager
in companies here in Italy, but I've

00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:50.115
always had a big passion for languages.

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:56.025
I've always studied foreign languages,
in school, but also on my own at home.

00:01:56.055 --> 00:01:58.275
So that was my big passion.

00:01:59.025 --> 00:02:03.525
And after 10 years working as an
export manager and having a lot of

00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:10.785
relationships with foreign subsidiaries
and with people abroad, I said, well,

00:02:11.535 --> 00:02:13.305
maybe there's another path for me.

00:02:14.655 --> 00:02:17.085
Foreign languages have
always been in my life.

00:02:17.145 --> 00:02:20.865
Even if I was doing a different
job, foreign languages were

00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:23.405
there, so that was a sign fpr me.

00:02:24.585 --> 00:02:29.625
And one day, my girlfriend, she is a
teacher as well in a school uh, she

00:02:29.625 --> 00:02:31.605
said, why don't you start teaching?

00:02:31.605 --> 00:02:35.115
I mean, you're, you're so
good at explaining things.

00:02:35.234 --> 00:02:37.575
You have a passion for foreign languages.

00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:40.275
Is it something that could
interest you, interest you?

00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:42.704
And I said, well, why not?

00:02:42.885 --> 00:02:44.055
I should give it a try.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:51.315
So, uh, I found a platform and I
applied and they accepted me and

00:02:51.315 --> 00:02:52.785
I started teaching as a tutor.

00:02:52.785 --> 00:02:56.025
So only conversation because I
wasn't qualified at the time.

00:02:57.615 --> 00:03:00.615
And after a few months I
realized that I liked it.

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:04.605
The number of students started growing.

00:03:04.605 --> 00:03:09.855
And then I decided to study and get a
few qualifications because I qualified...

00:03:09.915 --> 00:03:15.015
I got a qualification as a Italian
teacher and as an oral language coach.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:20.595
And so I said, that's about time.

00:03:21.045 --> 00:03:22.845
I need to change something.

00:03:22.875 --> 00:03:27.435
So I started my YouTube channel in 2019.

00:03:28.845 --> 00:03:31.065
The first year was pretty difficult.

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:39.285
No, I was making a lot of videos, a lot of
content, but the channel was not growing.

00:03:39.795 --> 00:03:42.840
And then after one year, It exploded.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.370
Like a lot of people arrived,
people were enjoying my content.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.420
And so I started improving my...

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.040
the quality of my content,
the quality of my videos.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:01.890
I started offering different content
like liturature, blogs, um, also

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.240
listening comprehension readers, because
I realized that people really enjoy it.

00:04:08.205 --> 00:04:08.695
And so...

00:04:08.965 --> 00:04:14.175
Sorry to interrupt...people always
accuse me of jumping in and not letting

00:04:14.175 --> 00:04:19.635
my guests speak but I have to say
that, first of all, you would say you

00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:21.045
were apologizing for your English.

00:04:21.085 --> 00:04:24.225
I just finished speaking with you
in Italian, and I can tell you that

00:04:24.225 --> 00:04:27.405
your English an awful lot better
than my Italian, and we will leave

00:04:27.405 --> 00:04:32.385
a link to, um, Francesco's channel
so that you can see some of his

00:04:32.385 --> 00:04:34.135
material also see our interview.

00:04:34.685 --> 00:04:37.665
Second of all, I think the greatest
qualification that you have as

00:04:37.665 --> 00:04:39.345
a teacher is your enthusiasm.

00:04:39.885 --> 00:04:45.195
And I was, um, I visited your
channel and when you talk about the

00:04:45.195 --> 00:04:47.695
cuisine and, uh, Emilia-Romagna.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:54.030
You talked about this very old tavern,
you know, dating back to the 14th century,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:59.490
uh, you make my mouth water for not
only the food, but for Italian culture

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.410
and everything that surrounds Italian.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:08.490
And I think the best teachers are the ones
that can motivate and generate enthusiasm,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.580
curiosity in their, in their students.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.050
And you not only generate that enthusiasm,
but you provide them with content

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.789
that you, that they can learn from.

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:19.249
So I just wanted to mention that.

00:05:19.749 --> 00:05:20.909
Phenomenal what you're doing.

00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:22.010
Sorry to interrupt.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.060
Thank you, Steve.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.500
And actually I think that studying
a foreign language is not just

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.280
about language itself, but
it's connected to everything.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.640
The culture leads you to the
history and also the food.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:36.210
Why not?

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.559
So that's why everything
is included in my videos.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:45.909
And maybe you heard my, you listen
to my last podcast maybe where,

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:51.190
where I mentioned, you know,
the, the oldest Osteria in Italy,

00:05:51.219 --> 00:05:52.630
which is in my town Ferrara.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:58.690
And I think I have the privilege of
living in a, in an amazing country where

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:04.289
people really can find everything, a
beautiful language, beautiful culture,

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.995
a love of history uh, good food.

00:06:08.745 --> 00:06:10.725
So I need it.

00:06:10.755 --> 00:06:14.445
I need to include it in my videos,
in what I'm telling people.

00:06:15.405 --> 00:06:17.145
So everything is culture.

00:06:17.145 --> 00:06:23.445
Everything is part of the
learning process of the student.

00:06:23.655 --> 00:06:24.405
Absolutely.

00:06:24.405 --> 00:06:29.234
I mean, I think back to when I was 17
years old, I was, we had French at school.

00:06:29.234 --> 00:06:30.435
I wasn't very interested.

00:06:30.614 --> 00:06:34.335
We had a French teacher again in
Montreal, all the French teachers were

00:06:34.335 --> 00:06:36.135
Anglophones until I got to university.

00:06:36.135 --> 00:06:40.094
We had a real live Frenchman and he
just turned me on to French culture.

00:06:40.364 --> 00:06:44.595
And as a result of that, then I
proceeded to learn other languages,

00:06:44.595 --> 00:06:49.935
but the role of a teacher primarily
to me is someone who can inspire

00:06:49.935 --> 00:06:55.185
an interest in the subject, uh, and
then, and then feed that, feed that.

00:06:55.185 --> 00:06:57.435
And that's exactly,
exactly what you're doing.

00:06:57.885 --> 00:07:02.385
Uh, so tell me a little bit about the
different parts of your, you know,  offer.

00:07:02.385 --> 00:07:03.135
What are the things...

00:07:03.164 --> 00:07:07.725
describe in some detail, your vlogs
here, your  or vlogs or your, your,

00:07:07.725 --> 00:07:12.690
your podcast, your, all the different
things that offer your subscribers.

00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:17.400
Uh, so when I started my channel,
I focused a lot on grammar.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:24.780
And then, um, I started teaching
grammar, but in context, I mean, I

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.859
don't start the video saying, okay,
let's start with the subjunctive,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.229
the conjugation is blah, blah, blah.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.900
I usually start with like an, like
a theme, sometimes a theatrical

00:07:36.969 --> 00:07:44.430
theme, like a very fun a fun scene
in context where I introduce the

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.190
grammatical aspect or whatever.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:56.280
So the student can start from, uh,
authentic or semi-authentic, uh, material.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.410
And from there he can understand
the grammatical aspect that

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.270
I'm, uh, that I'm explaining.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:03.870
Right.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.870
But then I said, well, the grammar...

00:08:06.405 --> 00:08:09.135
I mean, it's just part of the language.

00:08:09.195 --> 00:08:13.815
I mean, it's like an
accessory now of the language.

00:08:14.115 --> 00:08:15.795
So I said, I need more context.

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:17.535
I need more authentic materials.

00:08:17.895 --> 00:08:19.605
So I started vlogs.

00:08:20.415 --> 00:08:26.685
So when I'm like in Florence or
in Trentino or showing people

00:08:26.685 --> 00:08:30.735
around and explaining them what I'm
doing, what I'm seeing in Italian.

00:08:31.665 --> 00:08:37.814
And then I said, I started
reading articles and building like

00:08:37.814 --> 00:08:39.934
activities around the articles.

00:08:39.934 --> 00:08:45.525
So first, first listening comprehension,
and then the second listening

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:48.765
comprehension questions, vocabulary.

00:08:49.215 --> 00:08:54.015
And so trying to help students
understand on their own, the

00:08:54.015 --> 00:08:59.045
meaning of words or, or a specific
structure, starting from an article.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.440
And then one year ago I
started with literature.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:09.030
Literature is something that I
really love and actually it's, I'm

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.690
doing it because I like it because
I think it's very interesting.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.580
It's a good way to convey
the language to people.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.850
And it's something that I do
because I want to learn literature.

00:09:22.635 --> 00:09:26.385
It's a compromise, you know, I do it
for other people, but I do it for myself

00:09:26.385 --> 00:09:33.405
as well because I'm obliged, I'm forced
to study and to learn to make videos.

00:09:33.795 --> 00:09:36.405
You know, it's interesting in
literature because obviously

00:09:36.405 --> 00:09:37.875
people have different tastes.

00:09:37.935 --> 00:09:42.345
Uh, I'm not into science fiction
for example, uh, I personally like

00:09:42.465 --> 00:09:44.295
literature from the 19th century.

00:09:44.505 --> 00:09:49.005
So I listened to I promessi sposi,
which the average Italian school child

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:51.915
hates apparently, but to me it's exotic.

00:09:51.915 --> 00:09:53.295
It's it's Italy.

00:09:53.295 --> 00:09:53.865
It's far away.

00:09:54.615 --> 00:09:56.415
The 16th or 17th century.

00:09:56.925 --> 00:10:00.255
So, uh, you know, and that, but
then I read Alberto Moravia,

00:10:00.275 --> 00:10:01.695
I read one of his books.

00:10:01.725 --> 00:10:03.855
Uh, I can't remember the name of it now.

00:10:04.065 --> 00:10:07.605
So literature now that the, the
advantage of 19th century is that

00:10:07.605 --> 00:10:11.495
all the e-books are available free of
charge, so we can import them into LingQ.

00:10:11.925 --> 00:10:16.255
Uh, we'd pay for the audio book and we've
got learning material for a month or two.

00:10:16.555 --> 00:10:20.655
Modern literature it's more difficult
to do that because of copyright issues.

00:10:21.135 --> 00:10:25.815
Uh, when you say literature, like
what, what period typically do you

00:10:25.815 --> 00:10:27.465
introduce and is it short stories?

00:10:27.465 --> 00:10:29.175
Is it full length novels?

00:10:29.175 --> 00:10:30.195
Is it poetry?

00:10:30.435 --> 00:10:32.955
What is it that you
introduce to your learners?

00:10:33.255 --> 00:10:33.825
Okay.

00:10:33.855 --> 00:10:41.940
I made a video on poetry, but
usually it's, um, uh, you said that

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:43.400
you said a word that I forgot...

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.530
Short stories?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:44.940
No.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:45.480
Yeah.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.190
Like short stories or novels, sorry.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:47.550
Novels.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:47.790
Yeah.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.700
Novels from, yes.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:51.840
From last century.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.610
So 20th century.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.290
I started with Pirandello and then
now I'm working on Italo Calvino.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:10:59.860
Right.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:05.790
And so il narrotore has a
wonderful collection of audiobooks.

00:11:06.375 --> 00:11:09.255
Uh, Pirandello, Italo Calvino, et cetera.

00:11:09.255 --> 00:11:10.095
I have them at home.

00:11:10.095 --> 00:11:14.175
I have a large collection of audio
books produced by il narrotore.

00:11:15.805 --> 00:11:20.655
Yeah, there's a lot of content around,
but I wanted to, I wanted to offer content

00:11:20.685 --> 00:11:22.995
accessible to intermediate students.

00:11:23.025 --> 00:11:23.505
Right.

00:11:24.705 --> 00:11:26.955
Cause, you know, Pirandello is
very difficult to understand

00:11:26.955 --> 00:11:28.415
for a student of Italian.

00:11:28.814 --> 00:11:30.435
It's difficult for me as well.

00:11:30.585 --> 00:11:31.035
Yes.

00:11:32.564 --> 00:11:38.444
So I wanted, I wanted, I want it
to be accessible to intermediate

00:11:38.444 --> 00:11:40.064
students and that's what I'm doing now.

00:11:40.064 --> 00:11:45.255
So with Pirandello, Calvino, but
I made a video on Goldoni as well

00:11:45.285 --> 00:11:47.694
who lived in the 18th century.

00:11:47.715 --> 00:11:48.745
So that's the oldest.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:50.370
So...

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.820
but now, I'm focusing on
the 19th and 20th century.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:56.940
You know, just as an aside, one
thing that I've found extremely

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.760
valuable, uh, in my Persian learning.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:03.210
So I have a collaborator
in Iran who did sort of 26

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:04.860
episodes on the history of Iran.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.610
And after every episode, she
has these, what we call circling

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:09.960
questions, where she kind of...

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:14.640
the same vocabulary repeats three
times, and the same can be done

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.110
for literature and Iranian movies.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:17.520
Because in every...

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:22.589
in every country, it's not just Italy that
has wonderful food and wonderful history.

00:12:22.589 --> 00:12:26.969
And so every country has their thing,
you know, and very often it's not so

00:12:26.969 --> 00:12:29.939
easily, uh, understood or accessed.

00:12:30.239 --> 00:12:35.069
And if you get a little bit of a, of a
commentary, like from you explaining, and

00:12:35.229 --> 00:12:37.920
maybe using the vocabulary more than once.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.140
It then becomes easier to go in
and, and, and use these authentic

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.740
materials to improve not only our
understanding of the culture, but

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:48.870
also our ability in the language.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:53.610
I think that that's a tremendous thing
that you're doing for learners of Italian.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:55.890
]Thank you, Steve.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:13:01.000
Yes, I'm really focusing on, uh,
my followers needs, students'

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:03.150
needs, so I listen to them.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:08.310
I read their comments, I read the emails
and I, I try to you know, focus on

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:11.100
answering their questions, their needs.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.790
And I see that they really
love literature videos.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.070
They love listening comprehension videos.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:20.440
So I want to do more of that from now on.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:24.810
You know, I think it's tremendous too
that, that, uh, someone can engage with

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:29.370
you at your channel and tell you what
they want, what they like, what they

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.790
don't like, what they understand, what
they don't understand and get a response.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:36.220
If you buy a textbook, that's all
you got, you got the textbook.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:41.115
You can't ask the textbook anything, or
even if you buy a novel, but many of these

00:13:41.115 --> 00:13:42.465
language books, there's nothing there.

00:13:42.465 --> 00:13:43.845
They're just a lot of exercises.

00:13:43.845 --> 00:13:43.935
And...

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:49.709
whereas the kind of service that
is increasingly becoming available

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.479
via the internet from people like
yourselves, yourself, is this

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:58.079
ability to interact with people and,
uh, indicates what the needs are.

00:13:58.079 --> 00:14:01.560
And then for a person like you then to
respond to those needs, I think it's a,

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.599
it's a new world of language learning.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:07.560
So, um, I, can we, is there
something we should say at the end

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:09.270
here to summarize this discussion?

00:14:09.569 --> 00:14:11.760
Certainly I'm going to leave all
the links in the description.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.939
And, uh, Italian is such a
beautiful language to learn.

00:14:14.939 --> 00:14:19.709
My son, Mark, is very keen on Italian, is
learning italian or working on Italian.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.479
He played hockey in Asiago by
the way, uh, 20 years ago, uh,

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.180
for the, he played professional
hockey in Europe for 10 years.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.660
And one of the places he was is Asiagi.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.040
And so he's a great fan of Italian.

00:14:32.579 --> 00:14:36.540
Um, but, uh, is there anything that, so
we'll leave all those links, but is there

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:41.039
anything you would like to leave our
listeners with about, uh, about Italian?

00:14:41.839 --> 00:14:46.440
Well, uh, what I'd like to say is that
learning Italian, as I said before, it's

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:51.720
not just about learning the language,
not only learning the grammar, but

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:56.640
to really immerse in the language in
the, in the culture and everything.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:15:00.050
So that's why I've created
Vaporetto Italiano.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:05.760
I really want people to watch a
video and feel like they are in

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.070
Italy or really part of the country.

00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:10.365
Sounds like a good idea.

00:15:10.685 --> 00:15:13.195
And it's a great name by
the way, Vaporetto Italiano.

00:15:13.275 --> 00:15:14.055
Very clever name.

00:15:14.055 --> 00:15:16.305
I almost feel like I'm in Venice.

00:15:16.705 --> 00:15:17.445
Okay.

00:15:17.685 --> 00:15:20.175
Well, Francesco, thank you very
much for this interview and I hope

00:15:20.175 --> 00:15:24.735
